      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) as a master sensor and central regulator of energy metabolism has effects on various physiological, pathophysiological and nutritional events. Over the last few years, given the crucial nature of energy balance in the function of cells and whole-body, accumulating evidence has demonstrated that AMPK signaling has many implications for animal and human health with roles in diverse functions such as the synthesis of fatty acids, cholesterol, glucose, and hepatic gluconeogenesis and translation. Consequently, AMPK has been considered as a key therapeutic target for the prevention of diseases such as obesity, diabetes, cancer, inflammation and cardiac dysfunction. In this review we highlight recent advances on the upstream and downstream regulation mechanisms of AMPK, as well as the specific mechanisms involved in the regulation of AMPK on glucose, lipid, protein and other metabolic homeostasis. In addition, the potential application and perspectives of AMPK were also proposed."},
